1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,330
Forty crates of tomatoes.

2
00:00:02,970 --> 00:00:04,490
One cancelled restaurant order.

3
00:00:05,030 --> 00:00:10,250
A truck that leaves soon. Who takes the
stock now? This is SecondCrate,

4
00:00:11,150 --> 00:00:12,210
created by Shivam Gupta.

5
00:00:12,990 --> 00:00:16,750
It helps produce wholesalers turn
cancelled orders into confirmed sales

6
00:00:16,750 --> 00:00:18,490
through the buyers they already know.

7
00:00:19,450 --> 00:00:23,230
This is Northstar Produce, our fictional
demonstration wholesaler.

8
00:00:23,770 --> 00:00:26,010
I paste the cancellation and review the
lot:

9
00:00:26,650 --> 00:00:30,310
forty crates, an eighteen pound offer
and a sixteen pound minimum.

10
00:00:30,910 --> 00:00:35,690
The operator confirms the stock is available
and sets the dispatch and delivery times.

11
00:00:36,350 --> 00:00:39,570
The delivery promise stays visible
throughout the workflow.

12
00:00:40,617 --> 00:00:43,917
SecondCrate checks which buyers fit the
product,

13
00:00:44,277 --> 00:00:47,297
have permission to receive offers and
can take the stock.

14
00:00:47,997 --> 00:00:51,037
It prepares their conversations across
the configured channels.

15
00:00:51,917 --> 00:00:54,337
Every conversation shares the same
inventory.

16
00:00:55,257 --> 00:00:56,797
Buyers do not need another app.

17
00:00:58,283 --> 00:01:01,223
Maya asks for twelve crates at seventeen
pounds,

18
00:01:01,503 --> 00:01:03,203
provided delivery is before two.

19
00:01:03,883 --> 00:01:05,423
The agent interprets the request;

20
00:01:06,183 --> 00:01:08,163
application rules check the price,
quantity,

21
00:01:08,703 --> 00:01:11,683
capacity and delivery window. The order
is confirmed.

22
00:01:12,363 --> 00:01:14,823
Here is a fourteen pound request that
was rejected.

23
00:01:15,323 --> 00:01:17,403
The agent cannot negotiate away the
minimum.

24
00:01:18,817 --> 00:01:23,237
Ben takes twenty crates at eighteen
pounds. Eight remain.

25
00:01:23,897 --> 00:01:26,957
Now watch two buyers claim those final
eight at the same time.

26
00:01:27,517 --> 00:01:30,337
This button sends two concurrent
requests to the server.

27
00:01:31,017 --> 00:01:33,877
One succeeds. The other sees the
remaining availability.

28
00:01:34,737 --> 00:01:36,917
Forty crates never become forty eight
promises.

29
00:01:37,697 --> 00:01:40,237
Refresh the page, and that result is
still there.

30
00:01:41,517 --> 00:01:44,857
The ledger now shows three orders: forty
crates,

31
00:01:45,077 --> 00:01:49,697
two hundred kilograms allocated and seven
hundred and eight pounds in booked sales.

32
00:01:50,677 --> 00:01:52,657
Subtract four hundred and eighty pounds
in book cost,

33
00:01:52,657 --> 00:01:58,637
and the spread is two hundred and twenty eight
pounds, before handling, delivery and software.

34
00:01:59,717 --> 00:02:02,637
This recording uses the isolated
rehearsal.

35
00:02:03,557 --> 00:02:06,277
Messages are simulated, and the
application says so.

36
00:02:06,897 --> 00:02:11,037
The inventory decisions run against the
server and persist after refresh.

37
00:02:11,957 --> 00:02:16,237
AWS integrations are implemented, but
account activation is still

38
00:02:16,237 --> 00:02:19,417
blocking AWS deployment and live service
verification.

39
00:02:19,737 --> 00:02:22,537
This preview makes that boundary
visible.

40
00:02:23,683 --> 00:02:28,023
The proposed price is two hundred and
ninety nine pounds per depot each

41
00:02:28,023 --> 00:02:31,603
month. At five pounds contribution per
additional crate,

42
00:02:32,263 --> 00:02:33,843
sixty extra crates cover it.

43
00:02:34,463 --> 00:02:36,163
Paid pilots will test those assumptions.

44
00:02:37,083 --> 00:02:39,343
SecondCrate gives good stock another
buyer,

45
00:02:39,603 --> 00:02:41,643
and the depot a promise it can keep.
